My research: colour and sound through MIDI
Why MIDI? MIDI doesn't carry actual audio. It sends instructions like "play this note, this loud, for this long.
" That makes it a good bridge between the colour detection and the sound. The Raspberry Pi only needs to send a simple message, and a software instrument plays the sound.
Each MIDI note is a number from 0 to 127, and each one has a velocity (how hard the note is played) from 0 to 127. That makes it easy to map numbers from colour data onto it.

A possible mapping system: The simplest approach I found is to use HSB (hue, saturation, brightness) values instead of RGB, because they line up well with musical qualities:

Hue (the colour itself, 0–360°)
Pitch / note
EG : Divide the colour wheel into 12 sections of 30° each, one for each note of the chromatic scale

Saturation (how intense the colour is)
Velocity / volume
EG : A vivid bead plays louder, a muted one plays softer

Brightness (light or dark)
Octave
EG : Light colours play higher, dark colours play lower

Connecting it to Nadia's audio: The idea I mentioned in my last journal, converting the recording into a MIDI sequence, 
is possible with existing tools. Ableton Live has a "Convert Melody to MIDI" function, and Spotify has a free open-source tool called Basic Pitch
that turns audio recordings into MIDI notes. Once the melody from the recording is in MIDI form, each note could be assigned a colour. 
The tapestry of beads could then be "read" like a score, or a bead's colour could trigger the matching part of the song.

Technical pipeline (first idea):
A camera or colour sensor on the Raspberry Pi reads the bead's colour. A camera would use OpenCV in Python.
A sensor like the TCS34725 is another option.
A Python script converts the RGB reading to HSB and maps it to a MIDI note.
The mido Python library sends the MIDI message.
A software instrument or DAW (GarageBand, Ableton, etc.) plays the sound.

